syntax: read every digit of a short zsh positional parameter

zsh reads $10 as the tenth positional parameter, and $123x as ${123}x,
unlike POSIX shells, bash and mksh, where only the first digit belongs to
the parameter (zshparam(1), "Positional Parameters"):

	$ zsh -c 'echo $10' _ a b c d e f g h i j
	j
	$ bash -c 'echo $10' _ a b c d e f g h i j
	a0

LangZsh parsed $10 as $1 followed by the literal 0, so tools saw the
wrong parameter. With LangZsh, a short parameter expansion starting with
a digit now takes all following digits.

Reproducer, before this change: `echo $10` with LangZsh parsed into a
ParamExp for "1" followed by the literal "0".

